00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,680
Spoon or sboon? An interesting thing about liaisons is that so much of it has to do

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:08,560
with whether a consonant is voiced or not. The key thing to remember is that the

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:12,480
vocal cords don't like switching around at the midpoint. If the first consonant is

4
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:16,700
voiced, the next one will be as well. If the first one is unvoiced, the second one

5
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:22,240
will sound unvoiced no matter what you do. For example, say the word spoon. Now say

6
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:27,300
the word spoon. Hear how they sound the same? This is why I'd like you to always

7
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:32,220
convert the preposition to, to duh when you're speaking English, no matter what

8
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:36,660
comes before it. In the beginning, to get you used to the concept, we made a

9
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:40,740
distinction between tuh and duh, but now that your schwa is in place, use the

10
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:45,520
single duh sound everywhere, except at the very beginning of a sentence. After a

11
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:51,300
voiced sound, he had to do it. After an unvoiced sound, he got to do it, at the

12
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:56,580
beginning of a sentence, to be or not to be. To have your liaisons tested, call 800

13
00:00:56,580 --> 00:00:59,060
-457-4255.
